# PROPOSAL — NOT APPLIED. DO NOT auto-apply / auto-deploy. Orchestrator deploys after owner review.
#
# WHAT: Add a startup-grace guard to the DOWN/alert branch of each of the 7 NOTIFICATION-ONLY
# infrastructure watchdogs so they cannot FALSE-ALARM during the 15-min settling window right
# after Home Assistant (re)starts, when entities are transiently unavailable/unknown while
# integrations reload.
#   siren    1789300001001  (siren.alarm unavailable)
#   boiler   1789400001001  (binary_sensor.boiler_alarm on)
#   proxmox  1789500001001  (node mylab offline)         <- restart-sensitive
#   proxmox  1789500001002  (VM unexpectedly stopped)
#   proxmox  1789500001003  (storage >= 90%)
#   proxmox  1789500001004  (backup problem)
#   proxmox  1789500001005  (node metric stalled/unavail) <- restart-sensitive
#
# GUARD (one condition added per automation, on the state-based DOWN/alert branch only):
#     - condition: state
#       entity_id: input_boolean.ha_startup_grace
#       state: 'off'
# It re-uses the EXISTING helper input_boolean.ha_startup_grace, which automation 1789200001001
# sets ON at homeassistant.start and OFF after exactly 15 min (mode: restart). While the helper
# is ON the DOWN branch is suppressed; once it clears, real outages alert normally.
#
# WHY action-time (choose-branch) and NOT a top-level automation condition:
#   The guard is added INSIDE the choose branch's `conditions:` (AND'd with the existing
#   `{{ trigger.id == 'down'/'problem' }}` template). A `choose` branch condition is evaluated
#   when the action runs — i.e. AFTER the trigger's `for:` debounce has already elapsed — so a
#   genuine outage that persists past the 15-min grace window still passes the guard and alerts.
#   A top-level `condition:` was deliberately AVOIDED because it would also block the RECOVERY
#   branch. Recovery branches (trigger.id up / problem_ok / fresh) are left UNTOUCHED.
#
# WHICH branch per watchdog: exactly the ONE state-based DOWN/alert branch
#   (trigger.id == 'down', or 'problem' for the backup watchdog). The staleness branches that
#   fire on time / time_pattern (backup `stale` at 09:00/21:00; stale-metric `stale_check`
#   every /30) are NOT guarded and do NOT need it: they re-evaluate the REAL age
#   (last_backup / last_updated) at fire time and already reject unavailable/unknown, so a
#   restart cannot fabricate a stale reading. Leaving them unguarded keeps them re-alerting
#   after grace clears and keeps the change to exactly one condition per automation.
#
# EXACT BEHAVIOR / EDGE CASES:
#   * Normal restart, entity recovers within `for:`  -> DOWN never fires (unchanged).
#   * Slow integration reload, entity unavailable past `for:` but recovers within 15 min
#     -> DOWN branch fires but guard = suppressed (no false alarm). No genuine outage owed.
#   * Genuine outage that persists BEYOND the 15-min grace:
#       - `for:` >= 15 min (siren 15m; stale-metric down 30m): DOWN fires after grace already
#         off -> alerts normally.
#       - `for:` < 15 min (boiler 5m, node 5m, VM 2m, storage 10m, backup 10m): the single
#         debounced firing that lands inside grace is suppressed; a subsequent state change
#         (these entities flap) re-triggers and alerts after grace. This is the accepted
#         trade-off — identical to the project's leak automation, which already gates on this
#         same helper — chosen because the alternative (no guard) produces a GUARANTEED false
#         alarm on every restart. Real alerting after grace clears is NOT weakened.
#   * The two staleness checks (backup stale, stale-metric stale_check) re-alert automatically
#     after grace via their periodic time/time_pattern triggers.
#
# SHARED HELPER: read-only. input_boolean.ha_startup_grace is also read (via
# `is_state(...,'off')`) by the leak automations (ids around lines 33 / 1143 / 3497). This
# patch only READS the same helper as an additional condition; it never writes it and does not
# interfere with the leak logic or the grace manager (1789200001001).
#
# SAFETY: Still NOTIFICATION-ONLY. The only service call in every one of the 7 automations
# remains telegram_bot.send_message (chat_id 100000000). No device / Proxmox / button / switch
# / script / input_boolean *service* call is added — the helper is used only as a read condition.
# No id changed, no id added, no automation added or removed; each of the 7 gains exactly one
# condition. Verified by tests/test_watchdog_restart_guard.py.
#
# TARGET FILE ON HA: /config/automations.yaml (fetched read-only via `sudo cat`; 58 automations,
# trailing newline present). MODIFIES 7 existing blocks in place; adds no block.
#
# APPLY (after review, from a dir containing the fetched automations.yaml):
#   patch -p1 --dry-run < docs/audit/watchdog_restart_guard.patch   # verify
#   patch -p1          < docs/audit/watchdog_restart_guard.patch    # apply to working copy
#   # then, on HA: back up first, config-check, deploy, reload:
#   #   sudo cp /config/automations.yaml /config/automations.yaml.bak_wdgrace_$(date +%s)
#   #   (copy patched file to /config/automations.yaml)
#   #   POST /api/config/core/check_config     # sanity BEFORE reload
#   #   POST /api/services/automation/reload
#
# ROLLBACK:
#   patch -p1 -R < docs/audit/watchdog_restart_guard.patch          # revert working copy
#   # on HA: restore the .bak_wdgrace_* backup to /config/automations.yaml, then
#   #   POST /api/services/automation/reload
#
